---
<Base
  title="Best Washing Machines Under $1,000, and the One That Doesn't Smell — DealWillow"
  description="The best washing machines in the US right now: LG's WM4000H front-loader for most homes, GE's UltraFresh GFW655 if your laundry closet runs musty, and LG's WT7005CW if you'd rather never wipe a gasket."
>
  <main class="wrap article">
    <a class="crumb" href="/">&larr; All reviews</a>

    <div class="head">
      <span class="cat-tag">Laundry</span>
      <h1>Best Washing Machines Under $1,000, and the One That Doesn't Smell</h1>
      <p class="meta">Updated Nov 2026 · Prices checked Nov 24, 2026 · US models only</p>
    </div>

    <div class="lede">
      <p>
        <strong>For most households, the LG WM4000H is the washer to buy.</strong> It holds the
        highest overall score Consumer Reports gives any washer this year — 87 out of 100 across
        roughly 120 machines — and Wirecutter has kept it as the top pick through its August 2026
        update. It's a 4.5-cu.-ft. front-loader with a built-in heater, steam and a normal cycle CR
        clocked at 85 minutes, and it typically sells for $879 to $949. If your laundry closet has
        ever smelled like a wet dog, the <strong>GE GFW655</strong> is the one to look at: it runs a
        fan through the drum after every load and is the only machine here built specifically to
        beat gasket mildew. And if you want to load from the top and never think about a rubber seal
        again, the <strong>LG WT7005CW</strong> does that for $695.
      </p>
    </div>


    <h2 class="block-title">Everything we recommend</h2>
    <div class="picks">
      <a class="pick" href="#wm4000h">
        <span class="pick-tag">Best overall</span>
        <span class="pick-role">Top lab score, 85-minute normal cycle, stackable</span>
        <span class="pick-name">LG WM4000H</span>
        <span class="pick-price">$879 · LG.com</span>
      </a>
      <a class="pick" href="#gfw655">
        <span class="pick-tag alt">Best against mildew</span>
        <span class="pick-role">Vents the drum dry, 5 cu. ft., auto detergent</span>
        <span class="pick-name">GE GFW655SSVWW</span>
        <span class="pick-price">$848 · Home Depot</span>
      </a>
      <a class="pick" href="#wt7005cw">
        <span class="pick-tag">Best top-loader</span>
        <span class="pick-role">No gasket to wipe, 70-minute normal cycle</span>
        <span class="pick-name">LG WT7005CW</span>
        <span class="pick-price">$695 · appliance dealers</span>
      </a>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ 1. LG WM4000H ============ -->
    <section class="product" id="wm4000h">
      <h2 class="prod-role">Best overall</h2>
      <h3 class="prod-name">1. LG WM4000H (WM4000HWA)</h3>

      <div class="specs">
        <div><span>Type</span><b>Front-load, stackable</b></div>
        <div><span>Capacity</span><b>4.5 cu. ft.</b></div>
        <div><span>Max spin</span><b>1,300 rpm</b></div>
        <div><span>Cycles</span><b>12, plus downloadable</b></div>
        <div><span>Heater / steam</span><b>Yes / yes (Allergiene)</b></div>
        <div><span>CR overall score</span><b>87 — top of ~120 tested</b></div>
        <div><span>Normal cycle</span><b>85 min (CR lab)</b></div>
        <div><span>Warranty</span><b>1 yr; 10 yr on the motor</b></div>
      </div>

      <div class="proscons">
        <div class="pros">
          <p class="pc-title">Reasons to buy</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Highest overall score in Consumer Reports' 2026 washer ratings</li>
            <li>85-minute normal cycle — one of the shortest among front-loaders CR tested</li>
            <li>Built-in water heater and steam, so cold washes still clean</li>
            <li>Internal heater plus Allergiene cycle carries AAFA certification</li>
            <li>LG front-loaders logged a 2.7% first-year service rate at Yale Appliance</li>
            <li>Stackable, and only about 30 inches deep</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="cons">
          <p class="pc-title">Reasons to avoid</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Gasket upkeep is on you — front-loaders mold more than top-loaders</li>
            <li>LG's customer service draws more complaints than its hardware does</li>
            <li>ThinQ app and Wi-Fi pairing are the weakest part of the package</li>
            <li>Price swings $848–$979 depending on the week and the retailer</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="body">
        <p>
          Two independent labs landed on the same machine. Consumer Reports rates the WM4000HWA
          87 out of 100 — tied for the best overall score among the roughly 120 washers in its
          2026 ratings — with top marks for washing, energy use, water use and quiet operation.
          Wirecutter's August 2026 update calls it the best combination of speed and cleaning power
          in its tests, with "some of the shortest, most effective cleaning cycles available." Both
          outlets put the normal cycle around 85 minutes, which is quick for a front-loader and the
          main reason this machine doesn't turn laundry day into laundry weekend.
        </p>
        <p>
          The hardware that produces those numbers is straightforward: a 4.5-cu.-ft. drum, a
          1,300-rpm spin that pulls enough water out to shorten dryer time, and a built-in heater
          that lets the machine wash hot without relying on your water heater. The Allergiene cycle
          is certified by the Asthma and Allergy Foundation of America, which matters if there's a
          dust-mite allergy in the house. TurboWash 360 is LG's name for the recirculating spray
          that shortens cycles; count it as a convenience, not a cleaning miracle.
        </p>
        <p>
          Reliability is the quieter argument for this model. Yale Appliance, a Boston retailer that
          publishes its own service data, logged LG front-loaders at a 2.7% first-year service rate —
          the best figure for any washer brand it sells. LG's direct-drive motor carries a 10-year
          warranty, and the design removes the belt and pulley that fail on older machines. What LG
          does not have is a good reputation for support: Wirecutter notes plainly that LG's customer
          service is weaker than the brands it ranks below this one, and owner threads back that up.
        </p>
        <p>
          One number circulating online needs a flag. A widely shared mold-prevention roundup lists
          this model's top spin speed as 360 rpm and its one-star review share at 38%; LG's own spec
          sheet and Consumer Reports both put the spin at 1,300 rpm, and the review counts on the
          major retailers don't resemble those figures. Treat that listing as an error until someone
          documents otherwise.
        </p>
        <p><strong>The bottom line:</strong> the safest $900 in laundry — best lab scores, fast cycles, and a service record you can check, as long as you're willing to wipe a gasket once a week.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============ 2. GE GFW655 ============ -->
    <section class="product" id="gfw655">
      <h2 class="prod-role">Best against mildew</h2>
      <h3 class="prod-name">2. GE GFW655SSVWW</h3>

      <div class="specs">
        <div><span>Type</span><b>Front-load, stackable, reversible door</b></div>
        <div><span>Capacity</span><b>5.0 cu. ft.</b></div>
        <div><span>Odor system</span><b>UltraFresh Vent + OdorBlock</b></div>
        <div><span>Gasket</span><b>Microban-infused</b></div>
        <div><span>Detergent</span><b>SmartDispense, ~32 loads</b></div>
        <div><span>Cycles</span><b>12, incl. Sanitize + Allergen</b></div>
        <div><span>Steam / Wi-Fi</span><b>Yes / yes (SmartHQ)</b></div>
        <div><span>Warranty</span><b>1 year, parts and labor</b></div>
      </div>

      <div class="proscons">
        <div class="pros">
          <p class="pc-title">Reasons to buy</p>
          <ul>
            <li>The only mainstream washer that actively dries its own drum and gasket</li>
            <li>5.0 cu. ft. — half a cubic foot more than the LG, for less money right now</li>
            <li>Microban built into the gasket, dispenser, pump and drain hose</li>
            <li>GE ranked first in J.D. Power's 2026 front-load reliability study</li>
            <li>SmartDispense holds a 50-oz. bottle and doses by load weight</li>
            <li>You can run the dryer from the washer's panel when stacked</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="cons">
          <p class="pc-title">Reasons to avoid</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Slower: Wirecutter clocks it about 20 minutes behind the LG on a normal cycle</li>
            <li>Vent cycle runs up to 8 hours and needs the machine left powered on</li>
            <li>The SmartDispense drawer itself isn't vented and collects water</li>
            <li>Wirecutter rates its reliability outlook below the LG's</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="body">
        <p>
          Front-loaders clean better and use far less water than top-loaders, and then a meaningful
          share of them start to smell. Consumer Reports' member survey puts mold complaints at 13%
          among front-load owners versus 3% among high-efficiency top-load owners — that's the tax on
          the whole category, not on one brand. GE's answer is the UltraFresh Vent System: after a
          cycle, a fan pulls room air through the door vent while the drum turns, drying the gasket
          and tub. The company also infuses Microban into the gasket, dispenser, dispenser housing,
          drain pump, drain filter and drain hose, which is the inelegant but effective way to say
          "we treated the parts that get slimy."
        </p>
        <p>
          Owner reports suggest the mechanism does what the marketing claims. A four-month owner
          write-up on automaticwasher.org describes the interior — drum, gasket, door glass — coming
          out bone dry with the door kept shut, and notes the same person's previous LG still smelled
          musty despite the door prop. The same post flags the trade-off honestly: the vent cycle runs
          about eight hours, so it's a run-it-overnight feature, and the SmartDispense drawer has no
          venting of its own, so it needs pulling out and drying now and then. If the machine is on a
          switched outlet or a GFCI that trips, the vent doesn't run at all.
        </p>
        <p>
          Elsewhere it's a big, well-equipped washer rather than a fast one. The 5-cu.-ft. drum is the
          largest here, SmartDispense holds a full 50-oz. bottle (about 32 loads by GE's count), and
          you get steam, a Sanitize + Allergen cycle, Wi-Fi through SmartHQ and reversible doors. The
          cost is time: Wirecutter puts it roughly 20 minutes behind its top pick on a normal cycle
          and rates its long-term reliability outlook below LG's. GE's own brand story is stronger
          than that — first place in J.D. Power's 2026 front-load reliability study, at about 74
          problems per 100 machines — so the two sources disagree, and the honest read is that GE
          builds a dependable washer but this particular model hasn't earned LG's track record yet.
        </p>
        <p><strong>The bottom line:</strong> buy it if the smell is the reason you're shopping — it's the only washer here that treats mildew as a design problem instead of a maintenance chore.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============ 3. LG WT7005CW ============ -->
    <section class="product" id="wt7005cw">
      <h2 class="prod-role">Best top-loader</h2>
      <h3 class="prod-name">3. LG WT7005CW</h3>

      <div class="specs">
        <div><span>Type</span><b>Top-load agitator</b></div>
        <div><span>Capacity</span><b>4.3 cu. ft. (up to 20 lb.)</b></div>
        <div><span>Max spin</span><b>800 rpm</b></div>
        <div><span>Programs</span><b>8 cycles · 9 options</b></div>
        <div><span>Water factor</span><b>IWF 6.5 · IMEF 1.57</b></div>
        <div><span>Energy Star</span><b>No</b></div>
        <div><span>CR overall score</span><b>80</b></div>
        <div><span>Warranty</span><b>1 year, parts and labor</b></div>
      </div>

      <div class="proscons">
        <div class="pros">
          <p class="pc-title">Reasons to buy</p>
          <ul>
            <li>No gasket, no door seal, no mildew routine</li>
            <li>70-minute normal cycle — faster than either front-loader here</li>
            <li>Consumer Reports calls its washing performance near the top of the field</li>
            <li>CR rates noise and vibration very good; TrueBalance handles off-balance loads</li>
            <li>Deep Fill adds water on demand, which front-loaders won't do</li>
            <li>About $695 — roughly $200 less than the LG front-loader</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="cons">
          <p class="pc-title">Reasons to avoid</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Not gentle on clothes — CR scores it only adequate for fabric care</li>
            <li>Not Energy Star rated; an agitator uses far more water and power</li>
            <li>800-rpm spin leaves more water in the load, so the dryer works longer</li>
            <li>No steam, no heater, no Wi-Fi; 1-year warranty only</li>
            <li>28 inches deep and 45 inches tall — check the closet before you order</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="body">
        <p>
          The WT7005CW is the argument for giving up on front-loaders entirely. Consumer Reports
          scores it 80 overall, praises its washing performance as near the top of the field, and
          rates noise and vibration very good — unusual for an agitator machine, which normally
          announces itself through the floor. Its normal cycle runs 70 minutes, faster than either
          front-loader here, and there's no rubber seal to wipe, no door to prop open, and no
          eight-hour vent cycle to schedule. For a rental, a garage, or a household that will never
          read a maintenance schedule, that's worth more than a few points of efficiency.
        </p>
        <p>
          What you give up is fabric care and running cost. CR scores gentleness only adequate, which
          is the polite way of saying an agitator is harder on knits and on anything with elastic.
          The machine isn't Energy Star rated, and its published water factor of 6.5 is roughly
          double a good front-loader's — LG's own figures put the WM4000H around 105 kWh a year
          against roughly 240 for a basic top-loader. An 800-rpm spin is the other half of that: more
          water stays in the clothes, so the dryer runs longer and costs more than the sticker price
          suggests.
        </p>
        <p>
          Feature-wise it's honest rather than clever. Eight cycles, nine options, three wash
          temperatures, three spin speeds, ColdWash for cold-water washing, Deep Fill when you want
          the drum actually full, SmartDiagnosis for phone-based troubleshooting, and a stainless
          drum instead of porcelain. One-year parts and labor is the industry minimum, and it's worth
          noting that LG sells an almost identical WT6100CW for around $569 that Consumer Reports
          scores a point higher with a 60-minute cycle — if you don't need Deep Fill or the 4-way
          agitator, that's the better buy.
        </p>
        <p><strong>The bottom line:</strong> the washer for people who want clean clothes and zero maintenance rituals — just accept the extra wear and the bigger utility bill.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="sec">
      <h2 class="block-title">Also worth knowing</h2>
      <p>
        Three names that keep coming up and didn't make the cut. The <strong>Electrolux
        ELFW7637A</strong> ($949 at Home Depot) is Wirecutter's runner-up: reversible door, interior
        light, and a SmartBoost dispenser that premixes detergent, but its cycles run about 10 minutes
        longer and Electrolux's reliability record is weaker than LG's. The <strong>Maytag
        MHW5630HW</strong> ($749 at Home Depot, down from $921) led J.D. Power's 2026 front-load
        satisfaction study and came second on reliability, though it lacks this group's auto-dosing.
        And the <strong>LG WM3400CW</strong> ($729) is Consumer Reports' cheapest Smart Buy in
        front-loaders — but CR clocks its normal cycle at 105 minutes and only 80% of Home Depot
        reviewers would recommend it, with leaks the recurring complaint.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="sec">
      <h2 class="block-title">How we picked</h2>
      <p>
        The shortlist comes from Consumer Reports' 2026 washer ratings (roughly 120 machines) and
        Wirecutter's August 2026 guide, cross-checked against Yale Appliance's first-year service
        rates and J.D. Power's 2026 reliability study. Specs and prices were verified on LG's and GE's
        US product pages and at Home Depot on <strong>Nov 24, 2026</strong>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="sec">
      <h2 class="block-title">Before you buy: two things almost nobody tells you</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Measure depth and the doorway, not just the width.</strong> Every full-size washer
        here is 27 inches wide, which is the easy part. The front-loaders are about 30 inches deep
        before you add hoses and a few inches of clearance for the door to swing, and the GE is
        nearly 40 inches tall — stack a dryer on top and the controls end up around eye level. Also
        check the voltage: the washer runs on a standard 120V outlet, but if you're pairing it with a
        new electric dryer, that needs a 240V circuit, and swapping a gas dryer for electric means
        paying an electrician before delivery day.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>The gasket is the real cost of a front-loader.</strong> Consumer Reports' survey data
        puts mold complaints at 13% for front-load owners against 3% for high-efficiency top-load
        owners. The routine that prevents it takes about 15 minutes a month: leave the door ajar
        after every load, wipe the gasket dry, run a Tub Clean or self-clean cycle monthly, and use
        high-efficiency detergent at no more than a quarter cup. If that's a routine you know you
        won't keep, buy the GE with the vent system or the top-loader — not the cheapest front-loader
        on the floor.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>
</Base>

